{"id": "command-and-conquer-3-tiberium-wars", "title": "Command & Conquer 3: Tiberium Wars", "steam": 24790, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxmt"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 5, "last": "2026-10-04"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["A 2007 Direct3D 9 real-time strategy game. Reported perfect after 25 minutes on an M2 with the environment on DXMT on the Wine 11 engine r12. DXMT itself handles Direct3D 10 and 11 only, and on the Wine 11 engine Highball's automatic modes leave Direct3D 9 to Wine's own Direct3D (the rule the Fallout: New Vegas report confirmed in its log), so that is most likely what drew the game, an inference from the app's rule rather than from this reporter's log. On the default Wine 10 engine the same mode attaches DXVK's Direct3D 9 instead, and whether the game runs there was not reported. No frame rate was given and nothing was measured here."], "knownIssues": [], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/command-and-conquer-3-tiberium-wars.json"},
{"id": "command-and-conquer-generals-zero-hour", "title": "Command & Conquer Generals Zero Hour", "steam": 2732960, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": null, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["The game stopped with a message that it needs DirectX 8 or later. Generals shows that when it cannot use the screen resolution it picked, and Mac screens have sizes it does not expect. Setting Resolution = 1280 720 in Options.ini (Documents, Command and Conquer Generals Zero Hour Data, create the file with that line if it is missing) is the usual fix, and Emulate display mode changes on the game's page can help. GenLauncher, a mod installer, runs as a Windows program in the game's environment. Not confirmed by the reporter."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "'DirectX 8 or later required' at start (reported on an M2 Pro, macOS 27.0.1)", "cause": "the game cannot use the resolution it picked on a Mac screen", "fix": "Resolution = 1280 720 in Options.ini, untested here"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/command-and-conquer-generals-zero-hour.json"},
{"id": "command-and-conquer-red-alert-2-and-yuri-s-revenge", "title": "Command & Conquer Red Alert™ 2 and Yuri’s Revenge™", "steam": 2229850, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 3, "median": 1, "last": "2026-10-27"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["The game asks Windows for a display mode the Mac doesn't offer, Wine refuses, and the game stops with 'Error - unable to set video mode'. The reporter got it running with Emulate display mode changes on for the game (Wine pretends the mode changed and scales the picture) and the D3DMetal graphics mode, whose 32-bit DirectDraw is cnc-ddraw. Which of the two did it was not separated, so the page sets no mode. No measurement here yet."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "'Error - unable to set video mode' and the game doesn't start", "cause": "the game asks for a display mode the Mac doesn't offer, and Wine refuses the switch", "fix": "On the game's page, open Game settings and tick Emulate display mode changes. If it still stops, set the game's graphics mode to D3DMetal (Apple's DirectX), which runs the game's DirectDraw through cnc-ddraw. Both together worked for the reporter."}, {"symptom": "The CnCNet 5 Yuri's Revenge installer does not launch (M5, default engine r28, with .NET 4.8 installed)", "cause": "not diagnosed", "fix": "none known yet"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/command-and-conquer-red-alert-2-and-yuri-s-revenge.json"},
