{"id": "grand-theft-auto-iv", "title": "Grand Theft Auto IV: The Complete Edition", "steam": 12210, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxvk"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 5, "median": 4, "last": "2026-11-17"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported playable twice: 55 minutes on Wine's own Direct3D (M4 Pro, engine r3) and 60 minutes on an M4 with the environment on DXVK (engine r17). The second report saw black line segments flicker in a grid across the picture on Wine's Direct3D, which the game's Definition and Shadow settings did not change, brief stutter when turning the camera until -availablevidmem 4000 was added to the game's launch options, and a DualSense that the game only saw after the two WineBus values Highball's Controllers through SDL tweak sets. The row now points at DXVK, the mode of the longer and more detailed report, for a 2008 Direct3D 9 game. Needs the Rockstar Games Launcher for sign-in on Steam. A 9 minute report on an M5 with the CrossOver based engine rated it playable with workarounds, usually smooth with frequent stutters, once it was installed in the environment that has the Rockstar Games Launcher. No measurement here yet. An M5 played it to the first mission at a steady 60 fps on the Wine 11 engine r31, DXVK by the report, with black squares glitching in and out now and then (highball-db#1285)."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Black line segments in a grid pattern flicker across the picture in play on Wine's Direct3D (reported on an M4, engine r17)", "cause": "Not investigated here. The game's Definition and Shadow settings do not change it", "fix": "Set the game's graphics mode to DXVK, where the same reporter played an hour"}, {"symptom": "Brief stutter when moving the camera (reported on an M4, engine r17)", "cause": "Not investigated here. The game budgets video memory from what the driver reports", "fix": "The reporter added -availablevidmem 4000 to the game's launch options in Steam and the stutter stopped. Not applied by the row, one report so far"}, {"symptom": "A DualSense controller is not seen by the game (reported on an M4)", "cause": "Wine's controller driver reads the pad through the Mac's raw HID path as well as SDL, and some games only see it through SDL", "fix": "Apply the Controllers through SDL tweak from the environment's recipes (it sets Enable SDL and DisableHidraw under HKCU\\Software\\Wine\\WineBus, the two values the reporter set by hand), then restart the environment"}, {"symptom": "Black squares glitch in and out across the screen now and then (M5, DXVK by the report, Wine 11 r31, highball-db#1285)", "cause": "not diagnosed", "fix": "none known"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/grand-theft-auto-iv.json"},
{"id": "grand-theft-auto-san-andreas", "title": "Grand Theft Auto: San Andreas", "steam": 12120, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "wine"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 5, "last": "2026-10-02"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["The 2005 original, which Steam no longer sells (its store page says it is no longer available; Rockstar sells the Definitive Edition instead), so this row is for people who already own it. Reported playable on Wine's own Direct3D on the default engine after two changes: the game refuses to start without Wine's virtual desktop, and DXVK was very slow in the menus and in play where Wine's Direct3D ran well. Inside the virtual desktop window there is no real fullscreen. A DualSense controller worked after the two WineBus registry values that Highball's Controllers through SDL tweak sets. Nothing was measured here."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Fails to start with 'Cannot find 800x600x32 video mode' (reported on an M4, macOS 27.0, engine r17)", "cause": "Not investigated here. The game asks for an 800x600 32-bit display mode at startup and gets none it accepts without a virtual desktop", "fix": "Turn on Wine's virtual desktop for the game: under HKCU\\Software\\Wine\\Explorer set Desktop to Default, with a Desktops\\Default size matching the in-game resolution. The reporter did this by hand in the registry. There is no native fullscreen inside that window"}, {"symptom": "Very slow on DXVK, in the menus and in play (reported on an M4)", "cause": "Not investigated here", "fix": "Set the game's graphics mode to Wine's Direct3D (wined3d), which the reporter says runs well"}, {"symptom": "A DualSense controller is not seen by the game (reported on an M4)", "cause": "Wine's controller driver reads the pad through the Mac's raw HID path as well as SDL, and some games only see it through SDL", "fix": "Apply the Controllers through SDL tweak from the environment's recipes (it sets Enable SDL and DisableHidraw under HKCU\\Software\\Wine\\WineBus, the two values the reporter set by hand), then restart the environment"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/grand-theft-auto-san-andreas.json"},
{"id": "grand-theft-auto-san-andreas-the-definitive-edition", "title": "Grand Theft Auto: San Andreas – The Definitive Edition", "steam": 1547000, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 5, "last": "2026-11-07"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported perfect on an Apple M6 with DXMT on the Wine 11 engine r31 in a 4 minute session. The 2004 original has its own page (grand-theft-auto-san-andreas)."], "knownIssues": [], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/grand-theft-auto-san-andreas-the-definitive-edition.json"},
{"id": "grand-theft-auto-v", "title": "Grand Theft Auto V (Legacy)", "steam": 271590, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxmt"}, "antiCheat": {"names": ["BattlEye"], "verdict": "warning"}, "reports": {"count": 17, "median": 4, "last": "2026-11-16"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Story mode plays. Needs engine x64-crossover26.3-r5 or later and the DXMT renderer. Launch it from Steam: Steam starts the Rockstar Games Launcher, which signs in and hands off to the game. The row passes -nobattleye on the Steam command line, the story-mode path: without it a fresh launcher profile starts GTA5_BE.exe and BattlEye's installer asks for its kernel driver, which cannot run under Wine (seen on an M4, 2026-10-03). Checked again 2026-10-03 on an M4, macOS 27.0, engine x64-crossover26.3-r16 (the Wine 11 engine with the fixed DXMT), DXMT: the launcher signed in through the Steam entitlement and the game reached its display calibration screen five minutes after Play, nothing played past it that run.\n\nBoth of the things that used to stop this game are fixed. The launcher's sign-in window is a Chromium surface whose GPU process draws into another process's window, which rendered nothing until engine patch 0007 (cross-process child swapchains) plus DXMT_ALLOW_CROSS_PROCESS_SWAPCHAIN=1, both of which the rockstar recipe applies. And the sign-in itself was refused for reasons that looked like a network or account problem but were the Mac's clock: with the time zone set automatically, it signs in.\n\nGTA Online cannot work. BattlEye ships with the game (a BattlEye directory and GTA5_BE.exe) and is kernel-level anti-cheat, so online is out under Wine on any tool. Story mode is the whole claim here.\n\nCommunity reports of about 120 fps on an M1 Pro were measured on CrossOver's own tree, often with frame generation on. Our range overlaps at the top but the low end is 60, and the open world has not been measured at all, so treat the community figure as an upper bound rather than an expectation.\n\nA player report on 2026-09-24 rates it perfect after 36 minutes on an M1 with macOS 27.0, on the Wine 10 engine x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r5 rather than the Wine 11 one this entry says the launcher needs. That has not been checked here. One possible reason is a launcher that was already signed in, since the Wine 10 engine's problem was drawing the sign-in window, but that is a guess. Re-checked on 2026-10-05 on an M4 with the Wine 11 engine r15 and -nobattleye: launcher passed, the game opens on its first-run display calibration screen. On a base M1, udance4ever measured about 25 fps in the benchmark with settings.xml set to full screen at RefreshRate 30, and about 50 fps with RefreshRate 0 and Windowed 2, borderless (highball-db#72, 2026-10-09).\n\nThe Epic copy starts through the Rockstar launcher since Highball 0.10.15, which recognizes it as GTA V and starts it the way the launcher needs for an Epic license. Two players play it that way, one with the game's benchmark at 51 to 62 fps at 1080p on a base M1 (highball-db#415, highball#309). The other, on an M4, sees the frame rate swing up and down (highball-db#463).\n\nTwo things a player on an M1 MacBook Air found. A graphics change that restarts the renderer in game (Texture Quality, or Screen Type set to Fullscreen) leaves only the top-left quarter of the picture drawn until the game restarts, so change those settings with the game closed or restart it after. And the first load into story mode can stall for many minutes while the game window is not in front, because the game pauses itself without focus, so keep it in front or turn Pause Game On Focus Loss off. A DualSense or other PlayStation controller does not work in the game's menus, while an Xbox controller or a pad in XInput mode does (highball-db#72, #415). The M4 player's frame rate swings show in the Metal HUD's graph while play feels smooth at 50 to 60 fps in 1080p borderless. Exclusive fullscreen did not capture the mouse for them, and vehicle smoke showed purple lines on Very High that went away on High (highball-db#463). A player on an M2 Max sometimes sees the lighting turn far too bright, which a restart of the game fixes (highball-db#452). A player on an M3 Max with the Wine 11 engine r27 drove around for 46 minutes and saw weird texture artifacts, not described further (highball-db#622). The Epic copy was rated perfect on an M5 Pro with the Wine 11 engine and DXMT from a short session (highball-db#666). On an M1 Pro the Epic copy played 86 minutes, with frame drops when cutscenes or new areas load, cutscene audio sometimes running ahead of the video, and odd mouse movement at first (highball-db#671). Two more reports on the Wine 11 engine r31 with DXMT: the Epic copy played over 261 minutes on an M5, rated playable (highball-db#1265), and one perfect over 56 minutes on an M5 Pro (highball-db#1273).", "Asks for the Wine 11 engine r15 or newer since 2026-10-03 (was r5). On r11 to r14 the Rockstar Games Launcher installer stops at once, and an environment already on an older revision is not moved on its own, so raising the pin makes Play offer the newest revision to those environments. Everything installed stays.", "Needs engine x64-crossover26.3-r5 or later. Earlier engines cannot render the Rockstar launcher's sign-in window, which is a browser view drawn by a separate process, so the game can never start.", "Story mode only. GTA Online uses BattlEye, a kernel-level anti-cheat, which cannot run under Wine on any tool.", "If sign-in fails with 'we are unable to handle your request at this time', check the Mac's clock: System Settings, General, Date & Time, with the time zone set automatically. A wrong clock is refused by Rockstar and looks like an account or network problem."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "The Rockstar launcher shows a blank or black sign-in window and the game never starts.", "cause": "the sign-in is a Chromium surface drawn by a separate GPU process into the browser process's window. Wine returned nothing for it until engine patch 0007.", "fix": "use engine x64-crossover26.3-r5 or later with the DXMT renderer (the recipe asks for r15, where the launcher installer works too), and apply the rockstar recipe, which sets DXMT_ALLOW_CROSS_PROCESS_SWAPCHAIN=1."}, {"symptom": "Sign in fails with 'Sorry, we are unable to handle your request at this time' (error 1.000.7).", "cause": "the Mac's clock being wrong, not the network or the account. Rockstar rejects the request. A second cause, confirmed 2026-10-24 by a player in Turkey (highball#477, M1 Max, macOS 27.0.1, Wine 11 r27): Wine names the Windows time zone by taking the first one in its list with the Mac's offset, so a Mac set to Europe/Istanbul reports Arab Standard Time (Riyadh) and Rockstar refuses the sign in, while the website works in a Mac browser.", "fix": "System Settings, General, Date & Time, with the time zone set automatically using your location. For the Turkey case, with the environment stopped, delete the seven UTC+3 zones listed before Turkey Standard Time (Arab, Arabic, Belarus, E. Africa, Jordan, Russian, Syria) under HKLM\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Time Zones in the environment's registry, which makes Wine report Turkey Standard Time. An engine update brings them back, so redo it after one. A fix in the engine is planned."}, {"symptom": "The BattlEye Launcher window asks to install BattlEye (Install / Cancel) and the game never starts (seen on an M4, Wine 11 engine r16, 2026-10-03)", "cause": "a fresh Rockstar launcher profile starts the game through GTA5_BE.exe, BattlEye's wrapper, and BattlEye's kernel driver cannot run under Wine", "fix": "start the game with -nobattleye, which Highball's Play passes for this game (from the next version after 0.10.4). By hand, put -nobattleye in Steam's launch options for the game, or turn BattlEye off for GTA V in the Rockstar launcher's settings. Story mode only either way"}, {"symptom": "GTA Online will not connect.", "cause": "BattlEye, a kernel-level anti-cheat, which cannot run under Wine.", "fix": "none. Story mode only."}, {"symptom": "A DualSense or other PlayStation controller does not work in the game, while it works in other games in the same environment (reported on an M1, highball-db#72, highball-db#415)", "cause": "not investigated. The game reads controllers through XInput and DirectInput. The player traced it to the DirectInput side, since an 8BitDo pad in XInput mode works (highball-db#415), and the only HID error in the first log names an Apple device (vendor 004c), not the DualSense", "fix": "use an Xbox controller or a pad in XInput mode. For the Steam copy, starting the game from Steam with Steam Input on also works, with the game added to Steam as a non-Steam game and -nobattleye in its launch options (highball-db#72, 2026-10-07)"}, {"symptom": "The game starts at 800x600 in a window with low settings, or goes back to it after you set a resolution", "cause": "GTA V doesn't know the Apple GPU that DXMT presents, so on its first start it picks its lowest preset. It also resets to that preset whenever the graphics card it sees changes, for example with a settings.xml copied from Whisky or CrossOver, where D3DMetal presents an AMD card (udance4ever, highball-db#72).", "fix": "Set the resolution and quality once in the game's Graphics settings, they stay from then on. Don't copy settings.xml from another tool, or set the resolution again after doing it. Presenting an AMD card through DXMT_CONFIG made it worse in a test: the resolution list then held only the window size."}, {"symptom": "After a graphics change that restarts the renderer in game (Texture Quality, or Screen Type set to Fullscreen), only the top-left quarter of the picture shows, Retina on or off", "cause": "not investigated. Reported by a player on Discord on an M1 MacBook Air (2026-10-11)", "fix": "restart the game, which then draws correctly with the same settings, or change those settings with the game closed in Documents/Rockstar Games/GTA V/settings.xml"}, {"symptom": "The first load into story mode stalls for many minutes (about 18 in one report) while the game window is not in front", "cause": "the game pauses itself while it does not have focus (Pause Game On Focus Loss, PauseOnFocusLoss in settings.xml). Reported by a player on Discord on an M1 MacBook Air (2026-10-11)", "fix": "keep the game in front during the load, or turn Pause Game On Focus Loss off"}, {"symptom": "The lighting turns far too bright during play (M2 Max, highball-db#452)", "cause": "not diagnosed", "fix": "turn off the Steam overlay in game (Steam, Settings, In Game), which stopped it for the reporter. Setting Post FX to High in the game's graphics settings also helped (highball-db#452, 2026-10-19)"}, {"symptom": "Weird texture artifacts (M3 Max, macOS 26.5.2, DXMT, Wine 11 engine r27, highball-db#622)", "cause": "not measured, and not described further in the report", "fix": "none known"}, {"symptom": "The Rockstar launcher's sign-in window never appears after the launcher was used in another environment ('Timed out waiting for browser window' in socialclub_launcher.log, reported on an M4, macOS 27.0.1, highball#430)", "cause": "the launcher keeps its browser profile in Documents/Rockstar Games/Social Club/Launcher/Renderer, and every environment's Documents is the Mac's own Documents unless Keep game files inside this environment is on, so environments share that profile and one launcher could not read what another wrote. Found by Byron-aire", "fix": "with the launcher closed, rename that Renderer folder (the reporter added .bak) and press Play again, giving the sign-in window a minute or two to rebuild. Keep game files inside this environment keeps each environment's profile apart. The very first launch also updates the launcher and installs Social Club and can time out on that alone, so a second try often works"}, {"symptom": "Shift+Tab freezes the game, then it crashes (reported on an M3 Pro, Wine 11 r27, DXMT, highball-db#697)", "cause": "Shift+Tab opens Steam's overlay over the game; not diagnosed further", "fix": "don't press Shift+Tab. Untested: turn off the Steam Overlay for this game in Steam (the game's Properties, General)"}, {"symptom": "The Epic copy's first run needed the Rockstar Games Launcher started on its own first, to activate the Epic licence (M5, Wine 11 r29, DXMT). Until then the launcher can show Buy Now for GTA V although it is in the Epic library (M5, Wine 11 r31, highball-db#1265)", "cause": "not diagnosed", "fix": "connect Epic in Highball, install the Rockstar Games Launcher in the game's environment, start it by itself once, then Play (highball-db#1039, credit RicardoJardines, and highball-db#1265)"}, {"symptom": "The Rockstar launcher shows a blank sign-in window and the game never starts.", "cause": "the sign-in is a browser surface drawn by a separate GPU process into the launcher's window, which Wine returned nothing for until engine patch 0007.", "fix": "use engine x64-crossover26.3-r5 or later with the DXMT renderer. The rockstar recipe sets DXMT_ALLOW_CROSS_PROCESS_SWAPCHAIN=1, which is also required."}], "verified": {"date": "2026-09-09", "engine": "x64-crossover26.3-r15", "chip": "Apple M1 Pro"}, "source": "db/games/grand-theft-auto-v.json"},
{"id": "grand-theft-auto-v-enhanced", "title": "Grand Theft Auto V Enhanced", "steam": 3240220, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "d3dmetal"}, "antiCheat": {"names": ["BattlEye"], "verdict": "warning"}, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 1, "last": "2026-10-08"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["On M1 and M2 Macs it does not run in Highball, play GTA V Legacy instead, which runs there with DXMT. Every report of Enhanced starting comes from an M4 Pro or M5 Pro. vkd3d-proton is offered only on the arm64 preview engines, not the regular ones. Starts under vkd3d, refuses under D3DMetal, and neither finishes. The game needs Highball's Wine 11 engine (CrossOver 26.3 base) and two graphics modes in one process tree: the Rockstar Games Launcher signs in under DXMT, the game is DirectX 12 only. Under D3DMetal it quits at once with ERR_GFX_D3D_NOD3D12, and what causes that is NOT yet known: the game creates a D3D12 device, makes 137 capability queries that all succeed, creates a DXGI factory and enumerates an adapter, and then refuses. Under vkd3d it reaches the story-mode loading screen at 53-62 fps and then loses the GPU device because MoltenVK cannot translate some of its shaders to Metal. Turn BattlEye off in the launcher first (Settings > My installed games), its driver cannot load under Wine. GTA Online is out of reach regardless. If sign-in is refused with #1.000.7, set the Mac's clock (see the rockstar recipe)."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "ERR_GFX_D3D_NOD3D12 within seconds of starting, under the D3DMetal graphics mode", "cause": "cause not established on Apple M1 Pro hardware, and a long list of candidates has been excluded by measurement on 2026-09-08 (macOS 26.6.2, engine x64-crossover26.3-r4, D3DMetal 3.0 and Apple's D3DMetal 4.0b2 from Game Porting Toolkit 4). Excluded: the DirectX 12 Agility SDK loader (the game declares SDK 611 and never calls D3D12GetInterface; supplying it changed nothing); device creation (succeeds at 12_0 on the real adapter); capability values (137 CheckFeatureSupport calls all return S_OK, and forcing every one of them to report supported changes nothing); the advertised feature level (D3DMetal claims 12_2 and even accepts device creation there, but making it report and behave exactly like vkd3d, 12_0 with E_INVALIDARG above, changes nothing); ray tracing (D3DM_SUPPORT_DXR=1 raises the tier to 1.1, no change); the GPU identity presented (vendor, device and description overridden, no change); DXGI (factory, adapter enumeration, GetDesc3, outputs, tearing, video memory budget and swapchain creation in seven configurations all succeed, identically to vkd3d); Highball's own timestamp shim (removing it entirely changes nothing); and Metal 4 mode. The game creates a device, reads capabilities, creates no resource, raises no exception, and quits. Field reports are consistent with a hardware floor: it is reported working on M4 Pro and M5 Pro and never on M1 or M2, and it uses hardware ray tracing, which Apple GPUs only have from M3 onward (this Mac reports GPU family Apple 7). That is a correlation, not a proof, and it does not explain why the same Mac starts the game under vkd3d.", "fix": "none known. Not an app-level problem."}, {"symptom": "Under vkd3d it loads to a black screen with only the progress bar, then exits on its own near the end of the load", "cause": "MoltenVK cannot translate two classes of the game's shaders to Metal, and one of them is a deliberate upstream limitation rather than a bug. Measured 2026-09-08 on an M1 Pro, macOS 26.6.2, engine x64-crossover26.3-r4 with MoltenVK 1.4.1: 42 graphics pipelines fail with 'Component decoration is not supported in tessellation shaders' (84 occurrences, control and evaluation stages), and 27 compute pipelines fail with MSL compile errors on vkd3d's bindless descriptor arrays ('reinterpret_cast casts away qualifiers', 'indirection requires pointer operand'). The tessellation one is an explicit SPIRV_CROSS_THROW in SPIRV-Cross's spirv_msl.cpp, with the comment 'Technically tessellation as well, but it is too complicated to support', still present on main, so a newer MoltenVK does not fix it. Setting the game's Tessellation option to 0 cuts the graphics failures from 42 to 13 and avoids the device loss, but does not eliminate them: the game still builds tessellation pipelines for some passes. Stock MoltenVK cannot be substituted to test newer translation code, because the engine needs an x86_64 build carrying the MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT patch that lets it work behind Wine's winevulkan; upstream fat builds report no Vulkan 1.3 device and nothing starts.", "fix": "none at the app level. The tessellation half needs SPIRV-Cross to implement Component decoration for tessellation, which upstream has declined as too complicated. Turning the game's Tessellation setting to 0 reduces the failures and removes the device loss, but the run still does not complete."}, {"symptom": "The Rockstar launcher says DirectX 11.1 (12.0 Required) with ERR_SYS_SYSREQ_GPU (MacBook Air M2, DXMT and D3DMetal, highball#540)", "cause": "the launcher checks the DirectX level before the game, and past it the game quits with ERR_GFX_D3D_NOD3D12 on M1 and M2 under D3DMetal (cause not known)", "fix": "play GTA V Legacy on M1 and M2"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/grand-theft-auto-v-enhanced.json"},
{"id": "graveyard-keeper-2", "title": "Graveyard Keeper 2", "steam": 4358690, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "d3dmetal"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 4, "last": "2026-09-27"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Released September 2026. Reported playable on D3DMetal with minor frame drops while exploring the world; the reporter found DXVK much worse, with serious frame drops. Nothing was measured here."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Minor frame drops and stutter while exploring the world (reported on an M5 Pro, D3DMetal, engine r9).", "cause": "not measured.", "fix": "none known. DXVK is worse for it, per the same report."}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/graveyard-keeper-2.json"},
{"id": "green-hell", "title": "Green Hell", "steam": 815370, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxmt"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 2, "median": 4, "last": "2026-10-23"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported perfect on DXMT, the default mode, over 12 minutes on an M4 in exclusive full screen at 1920x1080 and 120 Hz, medium quality: 43 fps in dense jungle, 53 to 60 around the river, where 60 is the game's own cap. No stutter. Unity 2021.2, 64-bit, Direct3D 11. No measurement here yet. A second player on an M5 found it lagged on the default Wine 10 engine and played fine at high settings on the Wine 11 engine r26 with DXMT (highball-db#640). Not measured here."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Lags on the default Wine 10 engine (reported on an M5, macOS 27.0.1, DXMT, highball-db#640)", "cause": "not measured", "fix": "use the Wine 11 engine, the newest x64-crossover26.3 under the environment's Advanced, Engine. Both reports played well there, by the reporters"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/green-hell.json"},
