{"id": "onimusha-way-of-the-sword", "title": "Onimusha: Way of the Sword", "steam": 2638890, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 4, "last": "2026-11-03"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Capcom's RE Engine, DirectX 12, so it needs D3DMetal. Reported playable on an M1 Max with HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1, as for the demo and the benchmark, most likely with the reporter's own patch to Highball's DirectX 12 layer (highball#624), which Highball doesn't ship. On the stock layer Metal refuses one of the game's pipelines and no game window appears."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Unsupported graphics card error at start on M1 and M2 chips", "cause": "D3DMetal reports AtomicInt64OnDescriptorHeapResourceSupported as false there, and RE Engine requires it", "fix": "Wine 11 engine r25 or later with HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1, which has Highball's layer in front of D3DMetal answer yes (the check passes on an M1 Max, highball#624)"}, {"symptom": "After the graphics card check Metal refuses a pipeline ('output of type uint4 is not compatible with a MTLPixelFormatRGBA8Sint color attachment') and no game window appears (M1 Max, stock layer)", "cause": "D3DMetal turns the game's 8-bit integer render target formats into Metal's RGBA8Sint while the shader writes uint4 (the reporter's analysis, highball#624)", "fix": "none shipped. The reporter got past it by rewriting those formats in their own build of Highball's DirectX 12 layer"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/onimusha-way-of-the-sword.json"},
{"id": "onimusha-way-of-the-sword-benchmark", "title": "Onimusha: Way of the Sword Benchmark", "steam": 4196100, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "d3dmetal"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 3, "last": "2026-10-16"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Capcom's RE Engine, DirectX 12, so it needs D3DMetal. On M1 and M2 chips D3DMetal says no to the engine's check for 64-bit atomics on descriptor heap resources and the benchmark stops with an unsupported graphics card error. On the Wine 11 engine r25 (Highball 0.10.16) the variable HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1 makes Highball's DirectX 12 layer answer yes, and the benchmark completes on an M1 Pro: score 11,127 at 1800x1169, 31.9 fps on average, 1 per cent low 15.1, ray tracing off, upscaling off, frame generation off. The recipe sets the engine and the variable.", "Capcom's RE Engine asks DirectX 12 for 64-bit atomics on descriptor heap resources and refuses the graphics card without them. On M1 and M2 chips Apple's D3DMetal answers no, so this recipe moves the game to the Wine 11 engine r25 or later, whose DirectX 12 layer in front of D3DMetal answers yes when HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1 is set, and sets it. Confirmed by a player on an M1 Pro with the unmodified D3DMetal 3 (Highball 0.10.16). On an M4 D3DMetal answers yes by itself and the variable changes nothing."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Unsupported graphics card error at start on M1 and M2 chips", "cause": "D3DMetal reports AtomicInt64OnDescriptorHeapResourceSupported as false there, and RE Engine requires it", "fix": "the recipe: Wine 11 engine r25 or later with HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1, which has Highball's layer in front of D3DMetal answer yes (confirmed on an M1 Pro, highball-db#515). On an M4 D3DMetal answers yes by itself."}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/onimusha-way-of-the-sword-benchmark.json"},
{"id": "onimusha-way-of-the-sword-demo", "title": "Onimusha: Way of the Sword Demo", "steam": 3974650, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "d3dmetal"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 3, "last": "2026-10-11"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Capcom's RE Engine, DirectX 12. On an M1 Pro it stops with an unsupported graphics card error, because D3DMetal answers no to the game's check for 64-bit atomics on descriptor heap resources (D3D12 OPTIONS11), the same check that blocks Kunitsu-Gami. A player got it running, through its benchmark at 32 fps, by patching D3DMetal itself so the check says yes. Highball cannot ship a modified D3DMetal, so out of the box it does not start on an M1. On an M4 D3DMetal 3 already answers yes (measured here 2026-10-14 with a probe). On Highball's Wine 11 engine r25 (0.10.16) the variable HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1 makes Highball's DirectX 12 layer answer yes, and the demo reaches gameplay on the M1 Pro with the original D3DMetal (confirmed by the reporter, highball-db#450). The recipe sets both; the game's benchmark has its own page. PRAGMATA SKETCHBOOK's demo stops at the same check and ran with the same D3DMetal patch (highball-db#485). A second player played 58 minutes with the recipe. On Wine 11 Steam's licence agreement for the demo stayed invisible at the first launch, because Steam's window can't draw under D3DMetal there, and the game's graphics mode on DXMT for that one launch got past it (highball-db#450, see the known issues).", "Capcom's RE Engine asks DirectX 12 for 64-bit atomics on descriptor heap resources and refuses the graphics card without them. On M1 and M2 chips Apple's D3DMetal answers no, so this recipe moves the game to the Wine 11 engine r25 or later, whose DirectX 12 layer in front of D3DMetal answers yes when HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1 is set, and sets it. Confirmed by a player on an M1 Pro with the unmodified D3DMetal 3 (Highball 0.10.16). On an M4 D3DMetal answers yes by itself and the variable changes nothing."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Unsupported graphics card error at start, then the game quits (M1 Pro)", "cause": "D3DMetal reports AtomicInt64OnDescriptorHeapResourceSupported as false, and the game requires it", "fix": "the recipe: Wine 11 engine r25 or later with HB_TSSHIM_ATOMIC64=1, which has Highball's layer in front of D3DMetal answer yes (confirmed on an M1 Pro, highball-db#450)"}, {"symptom": "At the first launch nothing happens: Steam's window is black and the demo never starts (Wine 11 engine, D3DMetal)", "cause": "Steam waits for its licence agreement for the demo, and on the Wine 11 engines Steam's window can't draw under D3DMetal, so the agreement stays invisible", "fix": "the player's way (highball-db#450): set the game's graphics mode to DXMT on its page, press Play and accept the agreement in Steam's window, press Stop, set the mode back to the environment's and press Play again"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/onimusha-way-of-the-sword-demo.json"},
{"id": "oninaki-demo", "title": "ONINAKI Demo", "steam": 1047000, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 3, "last": "2026-11-03"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported playing well on an M1 with D3DMetal until a crash after about 15 minutes. A Unity game."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Crashes at the first Healing Point, about 15 minutes in, when Start is pressed (M1, D3DMetal, Wine 10 r29)", "cause": "not diagnosed. The backtrace shows a write to address 0 inside Wine's ntdll (+0x38314) while it was handling an exception, nested many times over", "fix": "none known"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/oninaki-demo.json"},
