{"id": "the-sims-2-legacy-collection", "title": "The Sims 2 Legacy Collection", "steam": 3314070, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "wine"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 5, "last": "2026-09-27"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported perfect on Wine's own Direct3D on the Wine 11 engine r11 with the frame rate limit at 60, after a short session on an M4. Nothing was measured here. A second M4 report: crashes loading a neighbourhood on 0.10.3, runs on 0.10.10 with default settings once some in-game settings are lowered."], "knownIssues": [], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/the-sims-2-legacy-collection.json"},
{"id": "the-sims-4", "title": "The Sims 4", "steam": 1222670, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxmt"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 4, "last": "2026-10-26"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Installed and played through the EA app on the Wine 11 (CrossOver 26.3) engine: 26 GB download, signed-in main menu, Create-A-Sim, the Willow Creek world map, and live mode on a lot (moved in, career panel, clock running) at 128-130 fps (7.8 ms frame, GPU 5.5 ms) at 1728x1117 on DXMT, D3D11 feature level 11_0. Needs the EA app recipe (cross-process swapchain flag, junction handling) and that engine; the EA app does not install on the bundled Wine 10 engine. First launch shows a black full-screen window for about two minutes before the intro. Starting the executable directly hands off to the EA app, which relaunches the game, so its graphics settings and the Metal HUD come from the EA app's process. The Steam-sold copy and the other renderers were not tried. The Steam copy works too, through the EA app that Steam installs inside the environment: on its first two launches the EA app came up but the game did not, and the third worked once the EA app showed the game in its own library, which it does after it links a Steam purchase to the EA account. The same player could not start the download with Highball's Install button and installed it from Steam's own window."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "After minimizing the game window, the mouse stays confined to part of the screen", "cause": "not diagnosed", "fix": "none known"}, {"symptom": "Steam copy: the first launches start the EA app but not the game", "cause": "the EA app has to link the Steam purchase to the EA account and list the game in its library first", "fix": "keep the EA app open until the game shows in its library, then press Play again"}], "verified": {"date": "2026-09-06", "engine": "x64-crossover26.3-r4", "chip": "M1 Pro"}, "source": "db/games/the-sims-4.json"},
{"id": "the-sims-legacy-collection", "title": "The Sims Legacy Collection", "steam": 3314060, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "dxvk"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 4, "median": 4, "last": "2026-11-06"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Re-verified on engine r2 on 2026-09-10: launches from Steam and reaches the neighbourhood view at 99.3 fps, 1728x1117, 2.8 ms of GPU per frame, presenting directly rather than through the compositor. No MoltenVK device losses in the run, so the shadow-import fix is holding. EA's 2025 re-release draws with Vulkan rather than Direct3D, so the renderer setting has no effect on it; the bottle's renderer is irrelevant (dxvk here). It needs engine x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r1 or newer plus the Sims recipe, which sets MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1. On older engines the window opens black with the game running behind it: the game is 32-bit, Wine imports its own host memory into MoltenVK, and Metal loses the device on the first frame (kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory, six times, then nothing). With the patched engine it plays: neighbourhood and lot load, Sims take orders, 65 fps at 1728x1117. Reported black-screen elsewhere on M4 and M5 machines (highball-db issue #7, KhronosGroup/MoltenVK#2766); the fix is not chip-specific. Run again on an M4 on 2026-10-03 (macOS 27.0, engine r19): with the recipe's variable in the process the neighbourhood and a lot draw correctly; without it the window is black and MoltenVK loses the device at the first frame, as the older-engine note above says. Re-checked on 2026-10-05 on an M4 with engine r21 (DEP kept on): the neighbourhood view draws as before. Live mode checked on 2026-10-06 on an M4 (macOS 27.0, r21, MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1): the tutorial family's lot with a Sim on screen, zoomed in with the clock running, once at 1440x1080 and once with Retina resolution on (a 3840x2160 screen for the game). The Sim drew correctly both times, no black figures or noise, no lost device in the log. The black Sims and missing portraits reported on M4s with engine r5 (highball-db#160, #174) did not reproduce; family portraits were not brought on screen.", "This game draws with Vulkan, not Direct3D, so the renderer setting does nothing for it. What it needs is engine x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r1 or newer, whose MoltenVK carries Highball's shadow-import patch, plus the MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1 this recipe sets. Without both, the window opens black: the game's Vulkan device is lost on its first frame and never recovers."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Sims drawn as black silhouettes on the lot, and in the neighbourhood view the family portrait is coloured noise and the lot preview a black block", "cause": "the noise in portraits and lot thumbnails came from MoltenVK's shadow import (MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1, which this game needs) never copying what the GPU drew back to the game, reproduced with a test program on 2026-10-07 and fixed in engines r24 and r25 (Highball 0.10.14). The black Sims on the lot were reported on engine r5 and not reproduced here. reported by two people, both on an M4 (M4 with macOS 27 and DXVK, M4 Pro with macOS 26.5.2 and DXMT), so it is not tied to one graphics mode. Not reproduced here on an M1 Pro, where the neighbourhood and an empty lot drew correctly at the default resolution on 2026-09-21; the two reporters describe it differently, one saying it shows at a high in-game resolution (2880x1620) once a house is built, the other that it shows from the start and survives a restart of the game. The pattern, small textures arriving as uninitialised memory, points at the host-memory import path this 32-bit Vulkan game needs (MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1). Checked on an M4 here on 2026-10-03 (macOS 27.0, engine x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r19, the recipe's MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1 confirmed in the process): the neighbourhood map, the Goth lot with its walls, floors and pool, and Buy mode all drew correctly, with no MoltenVK error over ten minutes. The sims themselves and a lot's family portrait were not reached in that scripted run, so the reported part is still neither reproduced nor ruled out on an M4.", "fix": "engine r24 or newer. Lot thumbnails saved while the bug was there come right once the lot is saved again (confirmed by the reporter on r29, highball-db#160). Sim portraits were still noise on r29 on 2026-11-03, all but one face; whether those are portraits saved before the fix or a part the fix doesn't cover is not known yet"}, {"symptom": "Black window on launch, game and audio running behind it", "cause": "32-bit game with a native Vulkan renderer: Wine's wow64 layer imports its own host memory into MoltenVK for host-visible allocations, and Metal fails every vkQueueSubmit over those no-copy pages with kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory (verified 2026-09-02 on macOS 26.6.2, M1 Pro; same signature as KhronosGroup/MoltenVK#2766 on an M5).", "fix": "Engine r1 or newer plus MVK_SHADOW_IMPORT=1 (this recipe). Verified: with the patched MoltenVK and the flag, zero device losses and the game plays; with the same build and the flag off, six device losses and a black screen."}], "verified": {"date": "2026-09-02", "engine": "x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r1", "chip": "Apple M1 Pro"}, "source": "db/games/the-sims-legacy-collection.json"},
