{"id": "velocity-ultra", "title": "Velocity Ultra", "steam": 244890, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 1, "median": 2, "last": "2026-10-15"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Launches, not playable. The game's pre-launcher (settings.exe, 32-bit) shows, and Play rarely brings the main window. The launch log has DXVK's 'Failed to create surface' twice and a stack overflow in settings.exe. A Direct3D 9 game, so in the D3DMetal and DXMT modes it goes through DXVK's Direct3D 9 on the default engine; With Wine's own Direct3D as the game's mode the pre-launcher crashes outright (0xc0000409, a fail-fast in settings.exe, before game.exe starts; highball-db#493, 2026-10-19, engine r28), so that mode is worse. ProtonDB has it working with GE-Proton on Linux."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "The main window rarely opens after the pre-launcher's Play, and when it does the ship moves very slowly", "cause": "not diagnosed; the log shows DXVK failing to create its presentation surface and a stack overflow in the pre-launcher", "fix": "none known. Wine's own Direct3D makes the pre-launcher crash (2026-10-19). Untested: starting game.exe directly, without settings.exe"}, {"symptom": "game.exe exits about two seconds in, or stops itself with a fatal error (0xc0000409), right after creating its Direct3D 9 device, on DXVK ('Failed to create surface') and on Wine's own Direct3D ('failed to set pixel format') alike (reported on an M1, engine r28, highball-db#493, 2026-10-21 and 23)", "cause": "both graphics paths fail at the same step, attaching the picture to the game's window, so it is how Wine's Mac driver handles that window at that moment rather than the graphics mode. It depends on timing, since the game starts once in a while", "fix": "none known. Needs work in Wine's Mac driver"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/velocity-ultra.json"},
{"id": "viewfinder", "title": "Viewfinder", "steam": 1382070, "status": "community", "backends": {"mac": "wine"}, "antiCheat": null, "reports": {"count": 3, "median": 5, "last": "2026-10-23"}, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Reported perfect on Wine's own Direct3D over 46 minutes. No measurement here yet. Perfect again on the Epic copy over 103 minutes, in a DXVK environment on an M2 with engine r28. A third player, on the Epic copy with DXMT on an M5, rated it 4 of 5 (highball-db#647): the first launch showed nothing until the game was stopped and started again, and placing a photo needed a mouse. Not measured here."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "The first launch shows nothing (reported on the Epic copy, M5, macOS 27.0.1, DXMT, highball-db#647)", "cause": "not known", "fix": "stop the game and start it again, by the reporter"}, {"symptom": "A photo cannot be placed without a mouse (reported on the Epic copy, M5, DXMT, highball-db#647)", "cause": "not known", "fix": "use a mouse, by the reporter"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/viewfinder.json"},
{"id": "vindictus", "title": "Vindictus", "steam": 212160, "status": "community", "backends": {}, "antiCheat": {"names": ["Nexon Game Security"], "verdict": "warning"}, "reports": null, "nativeMac": false, "optIn": false, "requires": [], "steps": [], "notes": ["Vindictus starts through Nexon's Steam connector (nxsteam.exe), a small Electron app that asks for a Nexon account and a server before the game opens. Highball adds Chromium's --in-process-gpu switch when you press Play, which makes the connector's window appear on the default engine. Measured here up to the Nexon sign-in screen only, the game past it is the reporter's. Checked here on 2026-10-05 on an M4 with engine r21: Steam Connector opens on Nexon's account prompt at 100 s, which is as far as a run without a login goes."], "knownIssues": [{"symptom": "Nothing appears after pressing Play on the default engine. Nexon's Steam connector runs in the background with no window", "cause": "The connector is an Electron app. Its separate GPU process draws into a window the main process owns, and Wine 10 cannot present across processes like that, so the window stays transparent (the Wine 11 engine carries a patch for it). Measured on an M4 with macOS 27.0 on 2026-10-01: invisible on engine r15, visible on r12, visible on both with --in-process-gpu", "fix": "Highball adds --in-process-gpu to the launch, which keeps the drawing inside the connector's own process. When starting from Steam's own window instead, put --in-process-gpu in the game's launch options (right-click Vindictus in Steam, Properties)"}], "verified": null, "source": "db/games/vindictus.json"},
